MoP Nanoparticles Supported on In-doped Porous Carbon: Outstanding Catalysts for High-efficient $\mathsf{c o}_{2}$ Electroreduction
$\mathsf{C O}_{2}$ is not only the main greenhouse gas, but also a cheap, abundant, and safe carbon resource. Producing fuels or chemicals using $\mathsf{C O}_{2}$ is an attractive way to achieve a carbonneutral energy cycle.[1,2] Among the methods for $\mathsf{C O}_{2}$ conversion, electrochemical reduction reaction systems are green, modular and easy for scale-up applications.[3,4] The electricity used to drive the processes can be generated from renewable energy sources, such as solar and wind.[4,5] Over the past decades, many metals, metal oxides and carbon-based materials have been used as electrodes for $\mathsf{C O}_{2}$ reduction in aqueous solution or ionic liquid (IL)-based solution.[6-16] Although these electrodes show competent $\mathsf{C O}_{2}$ reduction performances, they usually suffer from limited availability and low efficiency.
Recently, much attention has been paid to the development of non-noble electrocatalysts, such as alloys, metal chalcogenides, carbides, phosphides and boron nitride.[17-22] As typical representatives of the burgeoning non-noble electrocatalysts, transition-metal phosphides (TMPs), especially MoP with the bulk particles or amorphous structure, have known to be excellent catalyst for hydrogenevolution reaction (HER), oxygen reduction reaction (ORR) and direct methanol oxidization reaction (DMOR).[23,24] Using porous carbon materials to stabilize nanoparticles[19] is an efficient strategy to retain the highly activity of MoP.
Metal-organic frameworks (MOFs) are hybrid solids with infinite network structures built from inorganic connecting points and organic bridging ligands.[25,26] MOFs can be constructed from designable building blocks to impart unique properties for a wide range of potential applications including gas adsorption, separation, sensing and catalysis.[25,26] In addition, MOFs can act as the precursors to obtain porous carbon materials.[27]
Herein, we report the first work to use metal phosphide as the catalyst for $\mathsf{C O}_{2}$ electroreduction. It was discovered that the Faradaic efficiency of formic acid could reach $96.5\ \%$ with a current density of $43.8\ m A\ \mathsf{c m}^{-2}$ over the MoP nanoparticles supported on In-doped porous carbon $(\mathsf{M o P@{l n-P C}})$ . The current density is highest up to date with very high Faradaic efficiency. The high catalytic activity of supported MoP nanoparticles on porous carbon is attributed to more active sites for efficient $\mathsf{C O}_{2}$ adsorption and more space for electrolyte diffusion to the electrode than the bulk MoP. More importantly, the support can control MoP size and ensure the long-term stability. Besides, In species $\left(\mathsf{I n}_{2}\mathsf{O}_{3}\right)$ on the support could lead to stronger ${\mathsf{C O}}_{2}^{\cdots}$ adsorption on electrode surface. The reaction mechanism was studied in details on the basis of a series of control experiments.
The method to synthesize the MoP nanoparticles supported on porous carbon is shown in Figure S1 (Supporting Information, SI). In-MOF and Ga-MOF (MIL-68)[28] were selected as carbon precursors. Briefly, the initial MOFs were prepared by solvothermal reaction and the structures were confirmed by Xray diffraction XRD (Figure S2).[28] They exhibited a nubby structure and narrow size distribution (Figure S3-S4). According to the previous work, the vaporized ${\mathsf{M o O}}_{3}$ was first captured by the  pores of porous carbon derived from MOFs under the condition of chemical vapor deposition (CVD) to form Mo-based species.[20,29] Then, the adsorbed $M O O_{3}$ was reduced to obtain ${\mathsf{M o O}}_{2}$ nanocrystallites by the carbonization reaction to form ${\mathsf{M o O}}_{2}@\mathsf{I n}{\mathsf{-P C}}$ and $M_{0}\mathsf{O}_{2}@\mathsf{G a}.mathsf{P C}$ . More details about the characterization of ${\mathsf{M o O}}_{2}$ nanoparticles is given in SI (Figure S5- S9). Finally, MoP@In-PC and ${\mathsf{M o P@G a-P C}}$ (Ga-doped porous carbon) were generated by the reaction of ${\mathsf{M o O}}_{2}$ , $P H_{3}$ and ${\sf H}_{2}$ . Here, $P H_{3}$ was formed from the thermal decomposition of $(N H_{4})_{2}H P O_{4}$ at high temperature.[30] We also dispersed ${\mathsf{M o O}}_{2}@\mathsf{I n-P C}$ and $M\circ{\cal O}_{2}@{\sf G a-P C}$ into HF aqueous solution to remove In and Ga species, and obtained MoP@PC-1 and ${\mathsf{M o P@P C}}{\cdot}2$ by using the same method, respectively. It indicated that all carbon supports have average pore diameter of about $3.8~\mathsf{n m}$ and the surface Mo or $\mathsf{P}$ content in all catalysts were nearly the same with a Mo/P atomic ratio of about 1 (Table S1- S2).
Scanning electron microscopy (SEM) image in Figure 1A shows that densely packed MoP@In-PC were formed uniformly and they looked like nanosheets. Transmission electron microscopy (TEM) image in Figure 1B and high-resolution (HR) TEM image in Figure 1C clearly show that MoP nanoparticles were immobilized on the amorphous carbon matrix, and the size of MoP particles was about 4 nm. The MoP specific lattice spacing of (100) facet was $0.28\ \mathsf{n m}$ , which agrees with the reported value.[20] SEM and TEM images of MoP@Ga-PC, MoP@PC-1 and MoP@PC-2 are given in Figure S10-S11 and the morphologies were essentially the same. Elemental distribution mappings in Figure 1D illustrate that In dispersed predominantly within the carbon support. The atomic ratio of Mo and P is about 1, and In content was larger than Mo. This was consistent with the results from the XPS data in Table S2. X-ray diffraction (XRD) patterns in Figure S12 show that ${\mathsf{M o P@{\mathsf{I n}}{\mathsf{-P C}}}}$ was composed of MoP and $\mathsf{I n}_{2}\mathsf{O}_{3}$ on the support, and MoP@GaPC was composed of MoP and $G a_{2}O_{3}$ on the support. X-ray photoelectron spectroscopy (XPS) spectra in Figures 1E-1G further show the composition and chemical nature of MoP@InPC, including the peaks belonging to In oxide $(\mathsf{I n}_{2}\mathsf{O}_{3}$ : 444.5 eV), ${M}{\circ}^{\mathsf{N}}$ (Mo $3{\mathrm{d}}_{3/2}$ : $231.8~\mathsf{e V}$ ; Mo $3{\mathsf{d}}_{5/2}$ : $228.6\ \mathsf{e V},$ , $M\circ^{\vee1}$ (Mo $3{\mathsf{d}}_{3/2}$ : $235.7\:\mathsf{e V}$ ; Mo $3{\mathrm{d}}_{5/2}$ : 232.7 eV) and P ( $\textsf{P}2\mathsf{p}_{1/2}$ : $129.9\not\in\mathsf{V}$ ; $\mathsf{P}2\mathsf{p}_{3/2}\mathrm{.}$ 129.1 eV).[31-34] The Auger In MNN line position is observed at around $406.4\ \Lt\$ (Figure S13), which also suggests that In existed as oxide $\left(\mathsf{I n}_{2}\mathsf{O}_{3}\right)$ . $\mathsf{I n}^{0}$ did not exist since its characteristic feature $(\mathsf{I n}^{0};410.6\mathsf{e V})$ ) could not be detected. The other two Mo peaks of XPS were centred at 231.1 eV and $227.9~\mathsf{e V}$ , which can be ascribed to MoP.[35,36] Moreover, the peak at $133.8~\mathsf{e V}$ of $\textsf{P}2\mathsf{p}$ can be attributed to $\mathsf{P}_{2}\mathsf{O}_{5}$ or ${\mathsf{P O}}_{4}^{3}$ -,[20,34] which indicates that the surface of small MoP nanoparticles supported on the porous carbon was slightly oxidized, because of the high surface energy.[20,32] Analogous results can be seen in XPS spectra of MoP@Ga-PC (Figure S14). In addition, as measured by inductively coupled plasma optical emission spectroscopy (ICPOES), the actual In content in MoP@In-PC was $6.23~\mathsf{w t\%}$ and that in MoP@PC-1 was negeligible. Similarly, Ga in MoP@GaPC was also removed in HF aqueous solution when preparing MoP@PC-2. Their structures were quite similar to that of MoP@In-PC or MoP@Ga-PC except no $\mathsf{I n}_{2}\mathsf{O}_{3}$ or $\mathsf{G a}_{2}\mathsf{O}_{3}$ (from XRD patterns, Figure S15).
mappings of MoP@In-PC. The scale bar is $10\ \mathsf{n m}$ . E), F) and G) XPS spectra of In 3d, Mo 3d and P 2p orbits of MoP@In-PC, respectively.
To prepare the electrode, we dispersed the materials in acetone with Nafion D-521 to form homogeneous ink assisted by ultrasound, and then spread them onto carbon paper (CP). The catalytic performances for electroreduction of $\mathsf{C O}_{2}$ were studied. It has been reported that 1-butyl-3-methylimidazolium hexafluorophosphate $([\mathsf{B m i m}]\mathsf{P F}_{6})/\mathsf{M e C N}/\mathsf{H}_{2}\mathsf{O}$ containing $30\mathrm{\u}\mathrm{\%}$ [Bmim] ${\mathsf{{P F}}}_{6}$ , 65 wt% MeCN, and 5 wt% $H_{2}O$ was an efficent electrolyte for electrochemical reduction of $\mathsf{C O}_{2}$ to formic acid.[8] Thus the eletrolyte, [Bmim]PF6 (30 wt%)/MeCN/H2O $(5~\mathrm{{wt\%}})$ , was used in this work. As revealed by linear sweep voltammetry (LSV) in Figure 2A, MoP@In-PC showed the best $\mathsf{C O}_{2}$ catalytic activity among the five samples. A well-defined peak appears around $-2.2\vee$ (vs. ${\sf A g/A g^{+}}.$ with a current density of $50.2\mathsf{m A}$ cm2, and it was roughly 1.2, 2.8, 2.9 and 6.8 times higher than ${\mathsf{M o P@G a-P C}}$ , $M\circ\mathsf{P}@\mathsf{P}\mathsf{C}-1$ , ${\mathsf{M o P@P C}}{\cdot}2$ and bulk MoP, respectively. In addition, the much higher current density of $\mathsf{C O}_{2^{-}}$ saturated electrolyte than the ${\sf N}_{2}$ -saturated electrolyte in Figure S16 indicates the reduction of $\mathsf{C O}_{2}$ .
To further analyze the electroreduction products, controlled potential electrolysis of $\mathsf{C O}_{2}$ at each given potential in Figure 2B was performed in a typical $\mathsf{H}$ -type cell to quantify the liquid product by nuclear magnetic resonance (NMR) spectroscopy and the gas product by gas chromatography (GC). It indicated that formic acid, CO and ${\sf H}_{2}$ were the products with a combined Faradaic efficiency of around $100\%$ (Figure 2B and Figure S17). No other reduction product was detected. We also carried out the experiment using the same set-up and $6\mathfrak{h}$ electrolysis over MoP@In-PC using labeled $^{13}\mathsf{C O}_{2}$ . $^{13}\mathsf{C}$ NMR spectra showed an obvious peak at 162.1 parts per million, which can be attributed to $\mathsf{H}^{13}\mathsf{C O O}^{-}$ (Figure S18A). $^1{\sf H}$ NMR spectra of the product indicated that only $\mathsf{H}^{13}\mathsf{C O O}^{-}$ was produced (Figure S18B). These data confirm that ${\mathsf{C O}}_{2}$ was the only carbon source. Figure 2B shows the Faradaic efficiency for formic acid production over MoP nanoparticles on different supports and bulk MoP. It can be clearly observed that ${\mathsf{M o P@{\mathsf{I n}}{\mathsf{-P C}}}}$ had outstanding performance. The maximum Faradaic efficiency of formic acid occurred at – $2.2~\mathsf{V}$ (vs. ${\sf A g/A g^{+}},$ ), and it could reach $96.5\ \%$ with a current density of $43.8~\mathsf{m A}~\mathsf{c m}^{-2}$ . As far as we known, this is the highest current density with very high Faradaic efficiency.
Figures 2A-2C and Figure S19-S20 show that MoP@In-PC was more efficient than the others at all applied potentials studied, indicating that In was favorable to the electrochemical reaction. However, when In-PC, Ga-PC, PC-1, PC-2, ${\mathsf{M o O}}_{2}@{\mathsf{I n}}$ - PC and $M_{0}\mathsf{O}_{2}@\mathsf{G a}.\mathsf{P C}$ were used, both current density and Faradaic efficiency of formic acid were very low (Figure S21- S22). It means that MoP was essential for the high activity and selectivity. MoP exhibited a metallic character, since the valence band edge was located at ${\sim}0.15$ eV below the Fermi level (Figure S23). Photoluminescence (PL) technique was used to study the electron-transfer behavior. With incorporation of MoP into In-PC support, metallic MoP-semiconductor interfaces were formed and significant quenching of In-PC emission PL intensities could be observed (Figure S24). This indicates that the recombination of electron-hole pairs was suppressed and the separation of them was improved efficiently. Due to the good carrier mobility of MoP, highly active electrons on MoP surface participated in the reaction, leading to significantly enhanced formic acid production. We also used a series of MIL-68 with other metals including Al, Cu and Zn to prepare MoP@Metal-PC through the same method. Their electrocatalytic performances were not as good as MoP@In-PC (Figure S25).
We also used $[\mathsf{B}\mathsf{m i m}]\mathsf{P F}_{6}/\mathsf{M e C N}/\mathsf{H}_{2}\mathsf{O}$ with different compositions and some other common ILs as supporting electrolytes to perform the $\mathsf{C O}_{2}$ electrochemical reduction over MoP@In-PC, but their performances were not as good as [Bmim] ${\mathsf{I P F}}_{6}$ $(30~\mathsf{w t^{\circ}/o})/\mathsf{M e C N/H_{2}O}$ $(5~\mathrm{wt\%})$ system (Figure S26- S29 and Table S3). In general, the ILs composed of imidazolium cation and fluorine-containing anions were efficient supporting electrolytes. We also studied $\mathsf{C O}_{2}$ electrochemical reduction over ${\mathsf{M o P@{\mathsf{I n}}{\mathsf{-P C}}}}$ in 0.1 M $K H C O_{3}$ and KCl aqueous solution. It shows that the main product was ${\sf H}_{2}$ and the highest Faradaic efficiencies for formic acid were only 28.6 $\%$ and $25.9\%$ , indicating the importance of IL.
The electrochemical stability of the electrodes for $\mathsf{C O}_{2}$ reduction was tested at $-2.2\vee$ (vs. ${\mathsf{A g}}/{\mathsf{A g}}^{+})$ ) with an electrolysis time of $6\ h$ , and the total current density did not change obviously with time (Figure 2C). We also studied a long-term stability of ${\mathsf{M o P@{\mathsf{I n}}{\mathsf{-P C}}}}$ electrode with a $24\ h$ operation, during which there was no obvious decay in current density and Faradaic efficiency of formic acid (Figure S30), and activation period was not observed. The ${\mathsf{M o P@{\mathsf{I n}}{\mathsf{-P C}}}}$ electrodes before and after $24\ h$ electrolysis were characterized by XPS, SEM, TEM, LSV and electrochemical impedance spectroscopy (EIS) (Figures S31-S33). No obvious change in structure, morphology and electrochemical characteristics were observed, further showing the excellent stability of MoP@In-PC in the reaction. Consequently, MoP@In-PC can act as excellent catalysts for $\mathsf{C O}_{2}$ electroreduction with a superior formic acid production and a durative stability.
Besides, we determined the electrokinetic data in order to obtain further mechanistic insights of the $\mathsf{C O}_{2}$ reduction. The equilibrium potential for formic acid generation was $-1.88\vee$ vs. ${\sf A g/A g^{+}}$ , which was determined from an extrapolation method[37,38] using the current densities for formic acid at different potentials (Figure S34). Then, the overpotential for formic acid can be easily calculated. The Tafel plots were investigated to show the variation of overpotential with partial current density for formic acid production over these electrocatalysts (Figure 2D). The resulting Tafel slope of MoP@In-PC was $112.6~\mathsf{m V}~\mathsf{d e c}^{\cdot1}$ , smaller than that of MoP@Ga-PC, MoP@PC-1, MoP $\ @\mathsf{P}\mathsf{C}-2$ and bulk MoP, respectively. It exhibits a fast 1e- pre-equilibrium transfer to form ${\mathsf{C O}}_{2}^{\cdot\cdot}$ prior to a chemical $\mathsf{H}^{+}$ transfer step.[8,12,39] The others proceed an initial rate-limiting 1e transfer from $\mathsf{C O}_{2}$ to ${\mathsf{C O}}_{2}^{\cdot\cdot}$ that need higher overpotentials.
The excellent activity of ${\mathsf{M o P@{\mathsf{I n}}{\mathsf{-P C}}}}$ electrode may result partially from large $\mathsf{C O}_{2}$ adsorption capacity and electrochemical active surface area. According to the measured $\mathsf{C O}_{2}$ adsorption isotherms in Figure 3A, the $\mathsf{C O}_{2}$ adsorption capacity of the MoP@In-PC could reach $60.5~\mathsf{m g}~\mathsf{g}^{-1}$ at 1 atm, and it was roughly 1.4, 2.0, 2.2 and 15.7 times higher than that of MoP@Ga-PC, MoP@PC-1, MoP@PC-2 and bulk MoP, respectively. It indicated that In-PC played a key role for the large $\mathsf{C O}_{2}$ adsorption capacity. Moreover, the electrochemical activities of different MoP-based electrodes were characterized by single-sweep polarography. According to the Randles-Sevcik equation, the reduction current density at $-2.2\textsf{V}$ (vs. Ag/Ag+) plotted against the square root of scan rate are shown in Figure 3B. MoP@In-PC had a larger slope than the other catalysts, indicating the increased apparent concentration of reactant at the catalyst, leading to higher efficiency for $\mathsf{C O}_{2}$ electrochemical reduction.
EIS was conducted to investigate the properties of electrode/electrolyte interface. The Nyquist plots can be obtained by running the experiment at an open circuit potential (Figure 4A) and a simple equivalent circuit ${\mathsf{R}}({\mathsf{C R}}){\mathsf{W}}$ was then used to fit the high and medium frequency data (Figure S35). MoP@In-PC showed the lowest interfacial charge transfer resistance $(\mathsf{R}_{\mathsf{c t}})$ due to stronger interaction and superior cooperative effect between the nanosized MoP and In-PC. With a higher overall electronic conductivity, electron transfer to MoP@In-PC electrode surface in electrolyte is more facile than to other electrode surface. It could ensure a faster electron transfer to $\mathsf{C O}_{2}$ for forming reduced $\mathsf{C O}_{2}$ intermediate. As the step of $\mathsf{C O}_{2}\ +\ \mathsf{e}\ \to\mathsf{C O}_{2}\mathrm{\Delta}^{\cdot\cdot}$ is generally viewed as the ratedetermining step, the EIS results are in good agreement with the trend of Tafel plots.
Additionally, the stabilization of reduced ${\mathsf{C O}}_{2}^{\cdot\cdot}$ intermediate plays an important role in electrocatalytic conversion of $\mathsf{C O}_{2}$ to formic acid. In general, the overpotential of $\mathsf{O H}^{-}$ or ${\mathsf{S}}{\mathsf{O}}_{4}^{2\cdot}$ adsorption is considered as a measure of binding strength of intermediates in the electrochemical reaction.[11,12,40] So we examined ${\mathsf{S}}{\mathsf{O}}_{4}^{2-}$ adsorption as a surrogate for ${\mathsf{C O}}_{2}^{\cdot-}$ on the five electrodes by measuring single oxidative LSV scans between 0 and $1.7\mathrm{~V~}$ (vs. ${\sf H g}/{\sf H g}_{2}{\sf S O}_{4})$ at $10\mathrm{\mV\}\mathsf{s}^{-1}$ in ${\sf N}_{2}$ -saturated $0.1~\mathsf{M}$ ${\sf H}_{2}{\sf S}{\sf O}_{4}$ electrolyte, which could explore the binding affinity of the reduced intermediate ${\mathsf{C O}}_{2}^{\cdot}$ - during $\mathsf{C O}_{2}$ electrochemical reduction. The overpotential for surface ${\mathsf{S}}{\mathsf{O}}_{4}^{2-}$ adsorption on ${\mathsf{M o P@{\mathsf{I n}}{\mathsf{-P C}}}}$ was lower than the others as shown in Figure 4B. Lower overpotential indicates larger binding energy and stronger binding of ${\mathsf{S}}{\mathsf{O}}_{4}^{2-}$ , and hence MoP@In-PC could stabilize ${\mathsf{C O}}_{2}^{\cdot\cdot}$ intermediates more efficiently.[40] When In oxide (n-type semiconductor) was doped in the support, capturing quasi-free electron by $\mathsf{C O}_{2}$ becomes much easier, resulting in stronger ${\mathsf{C O}}_{2}^{\cdot\cdot}$ - adsorption on electrode surface.[41] The previous DFT calculation also suggested that In is a high-overpotential metal for ${\sf H}_{2}$ formation due to both local-geometric and local-electronic effect.[31] The results above demonstrate that MoP@In-PC had advantages in all the key steps for $\mathsf{C O}_{2}$ electroreduction to formic acid.
On the basis of the results and the knowledge in the literature, we proposed the possible pathway for the electroreduction of $\mathsf{C O}_{2}$ to formic acid using MoP@In-PC electrode. In the electrolysis, [Bmim]+ cation is electrostatically arranged at the MoP@In-PC surface, which favors $\mathsf{C O}_{2}$ adsorption via [Bmim- $\mathsf{C O}_{2}\mathsf{I}^{+}$ complex formation.[42-45] One electron was transferred to $\mathsf{C O}_{2}$ molecule to form $\mathsf{C O}_{2}^{\cdot-}$ intermediate. In this process, the reaction barrier may be also reduced with the aid of the formation of $[{\mathsf{B}}{\mathsf{m i m}}{\cdot}{\mathsf{C}}{\mathsf{O}}_{2}]^{+}$ , which could reduce the overpotential.[14] The protonation of ${\mathsf{C O}}_{2}^{\cdot-}$ may occur via capturing an electron-proton pair to form the adsorbed $H C O_{2}^{-}(H C O_{2}^{-}a d s)$ . Because the carbon support surfaces have low adsorption ability of $\mathsf{C O}_{2}$ reduction intermediates,[46] the integral $H C O_{2}^{-}\mathsf{\bar{a d s}}$ is released to form formic acid.
In summary, the electrocatalytic reduction of $\mathsf{C O}_{2}$ to formic acid was studied over MoP@In-PC, MoP@Ga-PC, MoP@PC-1, MoP@PC-2, and bulk MoP using [ $3\mathrm{mim}]\mathsf{P F}_{6}/\mathsf{M e C N}/\mathsf{H}_{2}\mathsf{O}$ as the electrolyte. It was found that MoP@In-PC was very efficient and stable electrocatalyst. The current density and Faradaic efficiency of formic acid could be as high as $43.8~\mathsf{m A}~\mathsf{c m}^{-2}$ and $96.5\ \%$ , respecrtively. The detailed study indicated that the excellent catalytic performance of MoP@In-PC can be attributed to the favorable character of nanoscale MoP, low interfacial charge transfer resistance, strong $\mathsf{C O}_{2}$ and ${\mathsf{C O}}_{2}^{\cdots}$ intermediate adsorption ability, as well as the synergistic effect between MoP and In-doped carbon supports. We believe that TMPs is very promising catalyst to replace expensive noble metals for $\mathsf{C O}_{2}$ electroreduction to other useful chemicals or fuels.
